begin model

#@title: |
#  Phage lambda lysis-lysogeny decision, detailed network-free model
#  (Cortes et al., 2017)

#@description: |
#  Network-free (NFsim / RuleMonkey) reformulation of the QSSA-reduced
#  "detailed" post-infection decision model of Cortes, Trinh, Zeng &
#  Balazsi (2017), Biophys J 113:2110-2120 (Supporting Material Eqs.
#  38-55). The circuit tracks the four decision proteins CI, Cro, CII
#  and Q, their transcripts (plus the paQ anti-Q small RNA), a
#  stochastically REPLICATING population of viral genomes DNA(), and a
#  cell that grows in volume. Fast dimerization, DNA-operator binding
#  and CII-FtsH binding are folded (QSSA) into mean-field promoter
#  occupancies that appear as functional transcription/replication
#  rates: pR (Cro/CII/Q operon) and pRM (CI) are governed by the
#  competing CI2/Cro2 occupancy F0/F_CI/F_CRO of pRM/R (Hill n=2 in
#  monomer, i.e. dimer control); pRE (CI) and paQ (anti-Q) are switched
#  on by the CII2 occupancy E_CII/A_CII (Hill n=2); CII degradation is
#  the FtsH-saturable rate d_CII = alpha_yf/(1 + kappa_FtsH*[CII]).
#  Concentrations are counts divided by the rescaled cell volume
#  v = Obs_Vol/Vscale, carried by a self-catalytic volume-proxy clock
#  Vol() growing exponentially v(t)=v0*exp(g t) (doubling time 100 min)
#  and capped at v=2; 1 molecule ~ 1 nM in an average cell (v=1).
#  DYNAMICS: transcription from pR makes Cro (a weak pRM/R repressor)
#  and Q rise first; a transient "bell-shaped" CII pulse (grows, then
#  is quenched by FtsH and dilution) can, at high enough per-cell viral
#  concentration m/v, activate pRE to boost CI above Cro and cross the
#  CI threshold -> LYSOGENY, while indirectly repressing Q via anti-Q
#  RNA interference. If instead Q crosses its threshold first, late
#  lytic genes fire -> LYSIS. The intrinsic discrete-molecule noise of
#  the SSA/NFsim trajectory replaces the paper's fitted Langevin term,
#  so the MOI-/volume-dependent probability of lysogeny (rising and
#  saturating toward 1 at high MOI) emerges first-principles from the
#  threshold-crossing race. The lysis-vs-lysogeny classifier is a
#  post-processing readout of the CI/CII/Q threshold crossings
#  (criteria #1/#2 of the SI); see run_cortes2017.py.

#@keyword: |
#  gene regulation, bacteriophage lambda, lysis-lysogeny decision,
#  cell-fate decision, threshold crossing, first-passage, bistable,
#  CII, Q antitermination, CI-Cro switch, viral DNA replication,
#  RNA interference, incoherent feedforward loop, network-free, NFsim,
#  Hill kinetics, stochastic gene expression, Escherichia coli

#@reference: |
#  Cortes MG, Trinh JT, Zeng L, Balazsi G (2017). Late-Arriving Signals
#  Contribute Less to Cell-Fate Decisions. Biophys J 113(9):2110-2120.
#  doi:10.1016/j.bpj.2017.09.012  (detailed model: Supporting Material
#  Sec. 1, reduced reactions Eqs. 38-55, parameters Tables S1-S3.)

#@note: |
#  Population/units convention. Interpretation is POPULATION-based
#  (molecule counts). Concentrations use the paper's rescaling
#  [X] = N / v in nM, where the rescaled volume v = Obs_Vol/Vscale and
#  1 molecule in an average cell (v=1) is approximated as 1 nM (the
#  paper takes Vbar ~ 1.5e-15 L so 1/(NA*Vbar) ~ 1.1 nM ~ 1 nM; the
#  Table S2/S3 fit uses the 1-nM/molecule approximation, which this
#  model reproduces exactly by writing [X] = Obs_X*Vscale/Obs_Vol).
#  NA and V_ref are provided for population conversion only.

#@note: |
#  Network-free engine notes. All functional rate laws are written as
#  SELF-CONTAINED expressions of Molecules-type observables and
#  parameters only (no function-of-function), because NFsim >=1.14
#  cannot evaluate an XML <Function> that references another
#  <Function>. The shared pRM/R partition-function denominator is
#  therefore repeated inline in rate_pR/rate_pRM/rate_repl. The volume
#  cap uses the comparison term (Obs_Vol<VolCap), which evaluates to
#  1.0/0.0 in BNG, NFsim and RuleMonkey. The reduced model is a finite
#  network (DNA + 5 RNAs + 4 proteins + clock), so ODE and generate-
#  first SSA are also feasible and are provided for verification; the
#  stochastic %lysogeny-vs-MOI result is produced network-free by the
#  driver. Bake MOI/v0 into the XML (text substitution) before an NFsim
#  run: post-init NFsim rate constants are not re-derived from changed
#  parameters.

begin parameters
  # --- Population-conversion constants (§1.3; used for [X] mapping only) ---
  NA      6.02214076e23  # /mol
  # Average E. coli cell volume Vbar used by the paper to rescale volume.
  V_ref   1.5e-15        # L/cell

  # --- Infection / cell-size inputs (overridden by the driver) ---
  # Multiplicity of infection = initial viral genome copies injected.
  MOI     1              # molecules
  # Rescaled initial cell volume at infection (0.5 small, 1 average, 1.5 large).
  v0      1.0            # dimensionless

  # --- Volume-proxy clock (carries v(t)=v0*exp(g t), capped at v=2) ---
  # Clock scale: Obs_Vol = v*Vscale. Large so the birth-process clock is
  # effectively deterministic (CV ~ 1/sqrt(Vscale)) yet cheap.
  Vscale  1000           # molecules per unit rescaled volume
  VolCap  2*Vscale       # clock ceiling at rescaled volume v=2
  # Exponential growth rate g = ln(2)/t_double, t_double = 100 min (Table S2).
  t_double 100           # min
  g       0.0069314718   # /min

  # --- Transcription rate constants (Table S2) ---
  # Rightwards transcription from pR (cro-cII-Q operon).
  k_R     1.1699         # /min
  # Transcription from pRM (CI maintenance).
  k_RM    0.2848         # /min
  # Transcription from pRE (CI establishment, CII-activated).
  k_RE    0.4938         # /min
  # Transcription from activated paQ (anti-Q sRNA, CII-activated).
  k_a     0.4386         # /min

  # --- Translation and RNA turnover (Table S2) ---
  sigma   0.6371         # /min
  d_r     0.2476         # /min
  # Anti-Q sRNA : Q-mRNA duplex-formation (RNA interference) rate.
  alpha_r 0.1544         # /nM/min

  # --- Protein turnover (Table S2) ---
  d_CI    0.0849         # /min
  d_Cro   0.002          # /min
  d_Q     0.00258        # /min
  # CII degradation is FtsH-saturable: d_CII = alpha_yf/(1 + kappa_FtsH*[CII]).
  alpha_yf   2.9464      # /min
  kappa_FtsH 1/5.0396    # /nM

  # --- Viral DNA replication (Table S2) ---
  # Theta-mode from free pR (omega) and Cro-bound pR (omega_Cro).
  omega     0.4045       # /min
  omega_Cro 0.021        # /min

  # --- Promoter occupancy constants (Table S3; single-site, dimer/Hill n=2) ---
  # pRM/R competitive occupancy by CI2 (kappa_CI) and Cro2 (kappa_Cro).
  kappa_CI  (1/5.84)^2   # /nM^2
  kappa_Cro (1/30.37)^2  # /nM^2
  # pRE activation by CII2.
  kappa_pRE (1/72.54)^2  # /nM^2
  # paQ activation by CII2.
  kappa_paQ (1/20.78)^2  # /nM^2

  # --- Decision thresholds (Table S2 / Fig. S4; read by the classifier) ---
  # Q lysis threshold; CI lysogeny threshold (criteria #1); CII lysogeny
  # threshold (criteria #2). Not used in any rate law.
  Q_T     134.897        # nM
  CI_T    61.0073        # nM
  CII_T   22.0           # nM
end parameters

begin molecule types
  # Viral genome copy; the shared template for all promoters and the unit
  # of theta/sigma-mode replication. Seeded at MOI, grows stochastically.
  DNA()
  # Decision proteins (phage lambda). Monomers; multimerization is QSSA.
  CI()
  Cro()
  CII()
  Q()
  # Transcripts. mRNA_antiQ is the paQ anti-Q small RNA.
  mRNA_CI()
  mRNA_Cro()
  mRNA_CII()
  mRNA_Q()
  mRNA_antiQ()
  # Volume-proxy clock particle (count/Vscale = rescaled cell volume v).
  Vol()
end molecule types

begin seed species
  DNA()         MOI       # molecules
  CI()          0         # molecules
  Cro()         0         # molecules
  CII()         0         # molecules
  Q()           0         # molecules
  mRNA_CI()     0         # molecules
  mRNA_Cro()    0         # molecules
  mRNA_CII()    0         # molecules
  mRNA_Q()      0         # molecules
  mRNA_antiQ()  0         # molecules
  Vol()         v0*Vscale # molecules
end seed species

begin observables
  # Decision proteins (counts; concentration [X]=Obs_X*Vscale/Obs_Vol nM).
  Molecules Obs_CI         CI()
  Molecules Obs_Cro        Cro()
  Molecules Obs_CII        CII()
  Molecules Obs_Q          Q()
  # Genome copy number and the volume clock (v = Obs_Vol/Vscale).
  Molecules Obs_DNA        DNA()
  Molecules Obs_Vol        Vol()
  # RNA-interference pair (insight into Q downregulation).
  Molecules Obs_mRNA_Q     mRNA_Q()
  Molecules Obs_mRNA_antiQ mRNA_antiQ()
end observables

begin functions
  # pR transcription: k_R x fraction of free pRM/R promoters, F0 =
  # 1/(1 + kappa_CI*[CI]^2 + kappa_Cro*[Cro]^2). [X] = Obs_X*Vscale/Obs_Vol.
  rate_pR() = k_R/(1 + kappa_CI*(Obs_CI*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2 + \
    kappa_Cro*(Obs_Cro*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2)

  # pRM transcription: k_RM x F_CI (fraction of pRM/R bound by CI2).
  rate_pRM() = k_RM*kappa_CI*(Obs_CI*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2/(1 + \
    kappa_CI*(Obs_CI*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2 + kappa_Cro*(Obs_Cro*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2)

  # pRE transcription: k_RE x E_CII (fraction of pRE activated by CII2).
  rate_pRE() = k_RE*kappa_pRE*(Obs_CII*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2/(1 + \
    kappa_pRE*(Obs_CII*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2)

  # paQ transcription: k_a x A_CII (fraction of paQ activated by CII2).
  rate_paQ() = k_a*kappa_paQ*(Obs_CII*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2/(1 + \
    kappa_paQ*(Obs_CII*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2)

  # Anti-Q : Q-mRNA duplex formation. Bimolecular rate constant alpha_r/v
  # gives propensity (alpha_r/v)*N_Q*N_antiQ (paper reaction rate alpha_r/V).
  rate_antiQ() = alpha_r*Vscale/Obs_Vol

  # FtsH-saturable CII degradation rate d_CII (QSSA of CII-FtsH binding).
  deg_CII() = alpha_yf/(1 + kappa_FtsH*Obs_CII*Vscale/Obs_Vol)

  # Viral replication rate = omega*F0 + omega_Cro*F_CRO (theta-mode from free
  # and Cro-bound pR); numerator/denominator inlined to avoid function-of-function.
  rate_repl() = (omega + omega_Cro*kappa_Cro*(Obs_Cro*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2)/(1 + \
    kappa_CI*(Obs_CI*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2 + kappa_Cro*(Obs_Cro*Vscale/Obs_Vol)^2)

  # Exponential volume growth, switched off once the clock reaches v=2.
  grow() = g*(Obs_Vol<VolCap)

  # --- Dimensional conversions (uncomment for nM concentration output) ---
# CI_conc()  = Obs_CI*Vscale/Obs_Vol   # nM
# Cro_conc() = Obs_Cro*Vscale/Obs_Vol  # nM
# CII_conc() = Obs_CII*Vscale/Obs_Vol  # nM
# Q_conc()   = Obs_Q*Vscale/Obs_Vol    # nM
end functions

begin reaction rules
  # --- Transcription (each viral genome is a template; rate = k x occupancy) ---
  # pR fires the cro-cII-Q operon (decoupled transcripts, SI assumption 3).
  R_txn_pR:  DNA() -> DNA() + mRNA_Cro() + mRNA_CII() + mRNA_Q()  rate_pR()
  # pRE and pRM both make CI mRNA (establishment vs maintenance).
  R_txn_pRE: DNA() -> DNA() + mRNA_CI()  rate_pRE()
  R_txn_pRM: DNA() -> DNA() + mRNA_CI()  rate_pRM()
  # paQ makes the anti-Q small RNA.
  R_txn_paQ: DNA() -> DNA() + mRNA_antiQ()  rate_paQ()

  # --- Translation (per transcript) ---
  R_tl_CI:  mRNA_CI()  -> mRNA_CI()  + CI()   sigma
  R_tl_Cro: mRNA_Cro() -> mRNA_Cro() + Cro()  sigma
  R_tl_CII: mRNA_CII() -> mRNA_CII() + CII()  sigma
  R_tl_Q:   mRNA_Q()   -> mRNA_Q()   + Q()    sigma

  # --- RNA interference: anti-Q sequesters and co-degrades Q mRNA ---
  R_antiQ: mRNA_Q() + mRNA_antiQ() -> 0  rate_antiQ() DeleteMolecules

  # --- Protein turnover (CII via FtsH-saturable functional rate) ---
  R_deg_CI:  CI()  -> 0  d_CI
  R_deg_Cro: Cro() -> 0  d_Cro
  R_deg_CII: CII() -> 0  deg_CII()
  R_deg_Q:   Q()   -> 0  d_Q

  # --- RNA turnover (anti-Q RNA has no first-order decay; SI Eqs. 51-54) ---
  R_deg_mRNA_CI:  mRNA_CI()  -> 0  d_r
  R_deg_mRNA_Cro: mRNA_Cro() -> 0  d_r
  R_deg_mRNA_CII: mRNA_CII() -> 0  d_r
  R_deg_mRNA_Q:   mRNA_Q()   -> 0  d_r

  # --- Viral DNA replication (theta-mode; grows the genome population) ---
  R_repl: DNA() -> DNA() + DNA()  rate_repl()

  # --- Cell-volume growth clock (self-catalytic; exponential, capped at v=2) ---
  R_grow: Vol() -> Vol() + Vol()  grow()
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
  # Reduced detailed model: a finite network (DNA + 5 RNAs + 4 proteins +
  # clock), so ODE / generate-first SSA / network-free NFsim are all feasible.
  # Two protocols are active (benchmarking, save/reset between them): a
  # deterministic ODE reference and a seeded single-cell SSA trajectory
  # (Fig. S2). The population %lysogeny-vs-MOI decision statistics (Fig. S3)
  # need many seeds + the threshold classifier and are produced network-free
  # by run_cortes2017.py; NFsim/RuleMonkey parity is checked there too.
  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 11 species, 19 reactions
  saveConcentrations()

  # Deterministic mean-field decision trajectory (average cell, MOI=1)

  #@protocol: |
  #  Integrate the reduced ODEs (SI Eqs. 38-55) for an average-sized cell
  #  (v0=1) at MOI=1 over the ~60 min decision window. Shows the
  #  characteristic transient "bell-shaped" CII pulse and the early rise of
  #  Cro and Q. This deterministic mean-field trajectory is the committed
  #  reference and is verified against an independent SciPy integration of
  #  Eqs. 38-55 in verify_cortes2017.ipynb (max rel err ~1e-6).

  resetConcentrations()
  simulate({method=>"ode",suffix=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>60,n_steps=>240})

  # Stochastic single-cell trajectory (seeded SSA; Fig. S2)

  #@protocol: |
  #  One stochastic realization (Gillespie SSA on the generated network).
  #  seed=>2017 yields a LYSOGENIC single cell (CI rises past its threshold,
  #  Q stays below its own) -- a Fig. S2 lysogenic trajectory. Individual
  #  runs are lysogenic or lytic; the average DNA copy number by 60 min is
  #  ~30 for lysogeny and ~100 for lysis (Fig. S2). The mean lysogenic and
  #  lytic trajectories are reproduced in verify_cortes2017.ipynb.

  #@figure: Fig. S2 in Cortes et al. (2017)

  resetConcentrations()
  simulate({method=>"ssa",suffix=>"ssa",t_start=>0,t_end=>60,n_steps=>240,\
    seed=>2017})

  # Network-free single-cell trajectory (NFsim, rejection sampling)

  #@protocol: |
  #  Same model simulated network-free (NFsim), equivalent to the SSA on
  #  this finite network. Used at scale by run_cortes2017.py for the
  #  decision statistics and NFsim/RuleMonkey parity checks.

#  resetConcentrations()
#  simulate({method=>"nf",suffix=>"nfr",t_start=>0,t_end=>60,n_steps=>240,\
#    gml=>2147483647})
end actions
